00:01:20.109 --> 00:01:23.369
And don't forget, it's not just people who
didn't receive direct deposit, but maybe people

00:01:23.369 --> 00:01:27.639
whose bank account information has changed,
whose addresses have changed.

00:01:27.639 --> 00:01:29.990
So none of that infrastructure is in place.

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:33.259
And like I said, I'd be very surprised if
that happens by next week.

00:01:33.259 --> 00:01:36.960
I think probably one to two months is more
likely.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:43.909
So two weeks ago, they said in three weeks,
the people who have the same direct deposit

00:01:43.909 --> 00:01:48.630
information as when they filed their tax returns
would start getting their twelve hundred dollars.

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:53.560
The people who need to, as you say, provide
banking information might have had a change

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:54.560
of address, et cetera.

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:57.299
It could be three more weeks.

00:01:57.299 --> 00:02:00.759
That sounds like you're saying it's all a
little bit optimistic right now.

00:02:00.759 --> 00:02:01.829
Well, all right.

00:02:01.829 --> 00:02:07.090
I mean, unless the IRS is going to start blindly
sending out twelve hundred dollars to bank

00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:11.810
accounts that, you know, just because something
was correct on a 2010 tax return doesn't mean

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:13.950
that bank account has some effect can be closed.

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:18.750
I mean, they could be sending twelve hundred
dollars really to a bank account that's not

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:21.040
open to someone else's bank account.

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:25.879
So I don't see how this is going to happen
unless people first have a chance to confirm

00:02:25.879 --> 00:02:31.489
that the information the IRS has is still
correct from the information the IRS had potentially

00:02:31.489 --> 00:02:33.300
a year and a half ago.

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:39.650
OK, so now let's talk about the the supplemented
the the both bolstered boosted unemployment.

00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:43.409
The idea was unemployment is, of course, managed
at the state level.

00:02:43.409 --> 00:02:45.019
Massachusetts has its system.

00:02:45.019 --> 00:02:47.319
Illinois has its system, et cetera.

00:02:47.319 --> 00:02:52.000
There are different state level benefits that
are the maximum benefits available to people.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:57.180
The federal government announced they would
be supplementing those benefits with up to

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:00.069
six hundred dollars per week in additional
funding.

00:03:00.069 --> 00:03:05.349
Now, I know a lot of people who have been
who filed for unemployment in the last three

00:03:05.349 --> 00:03:07.670
weeks, a 16 million people did.

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:13.989
They, as of now, are not getting anything
additional above and beyond what the state

00:03:13.989 --> 00:03:14.989
provides.

00:03:14.989 --> 00:03:17.750
What do we know about the rollout of that?

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:22.450
So as you just mentioned, everything is occurring
on a state by state basis.

00:03:22.450 --> 00:03:27.720
All I can tell you is there there's very there
was very little information, actually, about

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:28.909
what is happening.

00:03:28.909 --> 00:03:31.640
We know that there is supposed to be this
additional $600.

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:34.519
We don't know when it's going to be implemented.

00:03:34.519 --> 00:03:36.330
As you mentioned, each state has a different
system.

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:41.400
And on top of that, the care is also provided
that people who are self-employed, independent

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:45.849
contractors, for example, getting economy
workers would also be eligible for unemployment

00:03:45.849 --> 00:03:46.849
assistance.

00:03:46.849 --> 00:03:52.799
Now, typically, if someone is self-employed
or an independent contractor, there's typically

00:03:52.799 --> 00:03:57.939
a voluntary unemployment insurance program
that they can pay into to be eligible.

00:03:57.939 --> 00:04:00.129
So the CARES Act does away with that.

00:04:00.129 --> 00:04:03.390
But the problem is there is not a single state
yet.

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:08.389
As far as I'm aware, that has implemented
guidance as to how exactly this program will

00:04:08.389 --> 00:04:14.530
work for people in the gig economy, for example,
people who are self-employed and who is eligible

00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:17.180
and how they're going to get their money.

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:21.739
And right now, the guidance essentially is
for people to go out and apply and they'll

00:04:21.739 --> 00:04:24.220
try to figure it out later.

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:30.830
And of course, it is important for people
to get applications then, because the applications

00:04:30.830 --> 00:04:37.699
will be processed in the order that they received
and they will not get the unemployment insurance

00:04:37.699 --> 00:04:38.699
until.

00:04:38.699 --> 00:04:42.270
And so essentially for a period before they
apply.

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:47.099
So I know some people in the Midwest who you
know, there's a lot of states in the Midwest

00:04:47.099 --> 00:04:51.020
that have they don't have very robust unemployment
systems.

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:53.919
So they are maxing out sometime.

00:04:53.919 --> 00:04:56.490
Three hundred and fifty dollars a week.

00:04:56.490 --> 00:05:00.639
They're counting on that extra six hundred
that the federal government says is coming

00:05:00.639 --> 00:05:04.870
and it's not there and they're not getting
any answers from the state unemployment departments

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:06.800
as to when that's going to start.

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:11.400
Yeah, I'm in California, I can tell you, maxes
out at $450 per week.

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:16.310
So you can just think with our cost of living
in San Francisco and Los Angeles and San Diego,

00:05:16.310 --> 00:05:20.340
450 a week does not go very far at all.

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:26.500
And you're absolutely right that the six additional
six hundred dollars absolutely critical.

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:32.849
But the state employment offices are used
to processing a certain number of applications

00:05:32.849 --> 00:05:33.849
per week.

00:05:33.849 --> 00:05:39.370
And as you've pointed out, we've had an additional
50 and 60 million people applying for unemployment.

00:05:39.370 --> 00:05:44.419
And the infrastructure is just not there to
process those kinds of numbers.

00:05:44.419 --> 00:05:45.419
So let's now.

00:05:45.419 --> 00:05:46.419
Oh, sorry.

00:05:46.419 --> 00:05:47.419
Go ahead.

00:05:47.419 --> 00:05:48.419
Yeah.

00:05:48.419 --> 00:05:49.860
But Jason, there is one thing I wanted to
mention a lot of people are not aware of.

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:51.460
So this varies state by state.

00:05:51.460 --> 00:05:56.060
But a lot of people who are underemployed
right now, who have had their hours cut may

00:05:56.060 --> 00:06:00.620
be eligible for unemployment assistance again,
depending on the state, depending on what

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:02.870
their earning level is currently.

00:06:02.870 --> 00:06:07.590
So it's important for people who are underemployed,
who've had their hours cut to go ahead and

00:06:07.590 --> 00:06:10.570
apply for unemployment insurance as well.

00:06:10.570 --> 00:06:13.540
So then we've got the programs for businesses.

00:06:13.540 --> 00:06:17.050
And part of this is the payment protection
plan.

00:06:17.050 --> 00:06:22.419
There's a couple of other programs and elements
to these programs that are available every

00:06:22.419 --> 00:06:23.419
day.

00:06:23.419 --> 00:06:25.270
I'm sorry, what is it?

00:06:25.270 --> 00:06:26.289
Elements of medical.

00:06:26.289 --> 00:06:27.289
Elements of medical.

00:06:27.289 --> 00:06:28.289
Yes, elements.

00:06:28.289 --> 00:06:29.289
Elements of funding.

00:06:29.289 --> 00:06:33.800
So every day at a press cut press briefing,
Mike Pence or Donald Trump will say something

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:42.530
like, you know, a million people have applied
or three thousand lenders are now taking applications

00:06:42.530 --> 00:06:46.789
or we have processed 10 billion dollar every
day.

00:06:46.789 --> 00:06:50.060
It's different and the terminology being used
is different.

00:06:50.060 --> 00:06:53.030
Our business is actually getting money yet.

00:06:53.030 --> 00:06:56.539
Have businesses received any money yet under
that program?

00:06:56.539 --> 00:07:02.810
So I am not aware personally of any businesses
that who received money under the Paycheck

00:07:02.810 --> 00:07:10.100
Protection Program or the emergency or economic
injury disaster loan program.

00:07:10.100 --> 00:07:12.302
So that doesn't mean it hasn't happened.

00:07:12.302 --> 00:07:13.302
That just doesn't.

00:07:13.302 --> 00:07:16.830
It just means that the rollout has not been
very widespread.

00:07:16.830 --> 00:07:21.960
There are still some banks like Citibank,
which is my bank, that are not even accepting

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:26.370
applications and the money is already going
to run out.

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:32.840
I mean, Bank of America on its own, I think
as of a few days ago, they process, I think,

00:07:32.840 --> 00:07:37.509
over 20 percent of the total funding available
for the PDP program.

00:07:37.509 --> 00:07:40.180
Meanwhile, Citibank is not even accepting
applications.

00:07:40.180 --> 00:07:46.389
So the whole again, the rollout here has been
unfortunately a little bungled.

00:07:46.389 --> 00:07:48.780
It's increasingly seeming to me.

00:07:48.780 --> 00:07:55.020
And again, I hope that things can get reopened
sooner than later as it's safe.

00:07:55.020 --> 00:07:56.939
Never beyond what is safe.

00:07:56.939 --> 00:08:00.439
But I hope things get going as soon as this
is safe.

00:08:00.439 --> 00:08:06.699
It's starting to seem like a lot of these
funds may not even get to people before the

00:08:06.699 --> 00:08:10.729
reopening starts if things continue on this
path.

00:08:10.729 --> 00:08:17.509
Yeah, I mean, you know, it's not just a question
of reopening, but but by the time we're through

00:08:17.509 --> 00:08:23.680
with the lockdown, frankly, there will be
so much job loss.

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:28.520
And unfortunately, the job loss really is
not just affecting at this point the hospitality

00:08:28.520 --> 00:08:32.520
industries or the restaurant industries or
economic industries, but it's affecting all

00:08:32.520 --> 00:08:33.740
the other industries indirectly.

00:08:33.740 --> 00:08:38.210
I mean, I can tell you as a lawyer, for example,
the courts are closed.

00:08:38.210 --> 00:08:44.110
So any laws were in litigation have no work
right now, even if they did.

00:08:44.110 --> 00:08:45.790
Businesses don't really want to be spending
